})|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{3}-{\\left(\\frac{14}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186581655 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}\\right)|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{32}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}\\right)|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{3}-{\\left(\\frac{14}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186581657 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operator