l vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/71/71912c1b3d6fbd787a34fa6b5b1829ce0a4e1f6c8e12f25430e18e7c525f5492.png)\n\n  \n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}x([2\\sqrt{x}]-[x])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}x(2\\sqrt{x}-x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2{x}^{3/2}-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\left[\\left(\\frac{128}{5}-\\frac{64}{3}\\right)-0\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186585032 1071 "Compute $\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{2}X]$ if $\\text{E}[X]=6.$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186585032 1071 "Compute $\\text{E}\\left[\\frac{1}{2}X\\right]$ if $\\text{E}[X]=6.$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186585032 1074 "For any constant $a$ and any random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[aX]=a\\text{E}[X].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{2}X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(6) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186585032 1074 "For any constant $a$ and any random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[aX]=a\\text{E}[X].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}\\left[\\frac{1}{2}X\\right] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(6) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186585033 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+{60}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186585033 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+{60}^{∘}\\right),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186585033 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+{60}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{2}+{60}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+{60}^{∘}\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+{60}^{∘} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & \\ne {30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & \\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186585033 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+{60}^{∘}\\right),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+{60}^{∘}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+{60}^{∘}\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+{60}^{∘} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & \\ne {30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & \\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186585035 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2}-1}}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{′} \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-36}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186585035 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(12\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2}-1}}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{′} \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-36}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186585036 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{10x}$ on the interval $-\\infty <x<0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{10x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{10x}\\,\\text{d}x &={[\\frac{k}{10}{e}^{10x}]}_{-\\infty }^{0}=\\frac{k}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{10}=1\\,⟹\\,k=10.\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186585036 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{10x}$ on the interval $-\\infty <x<0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{10x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{10x}\\,\\text{d}x &={\\left[\\frac{k}{10}{e}^{10x}\\right]}_{-\\infty }^{0}=\\frac{k}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{10}=1\\,⟹\\,k=10.\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186585037 1071 "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-{40}^{∘})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186585037 1071 "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}-{40}^{∘}\\right)?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186585046 1074 "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-4$ and $\\sigma =1.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-4.47) & =P(Z<\\frac{-4.47-(-4)}{1}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.47)