ue of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{t}^{2},2⟩ & =0 \\\\ (-1)(-2{t}^{2})+(-1)(2) & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2(t+1)(t-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=\\pm 1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{2(-1)^{3}}{3},2(-1)-1\\right\\rangle =\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3},-3\\right\\rangle , \\\\ r(1) & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{3},2(1)-1\\right\\rangle =\\left\\langle -\\frac{2}{3},1\\right\\rangle ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $\\left(-\\frac{2}{3},1\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{2}{3},-3\\right).$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186585055 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{t}^{2},2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $⟨-1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{t}^{2},2⟩ & =0 \\\\ (-1)(-2{t}^{2})+(-1)(2) & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2(t+1)(t-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=\\pm 1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-\\frac{2(-1)^{3}}{3},2(-1)-1⟩=⟨\\frac{2}{3},-3⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨-\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{3},2(1)-1⟩=⟨-\\frac{2}{3},1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $(-\\frac{2}{3},1)$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},-3).$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186585061 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The range of the fu