ndom variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{2}],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1) & =P(1\\le X\\le \\frac{9}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9/2}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2}-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186589979 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $\\left[\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{2}\\right],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1) & =P\\left(1\\le X\\le \\frac{9}{2}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9/2}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}-1\\right)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186589980 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}