=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}} &=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{1}} &=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}} & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}(1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\