\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=\\left({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}\\right)+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}\\right)+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}} & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}\n$$ is true for all $n\\ge 1.$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186594228 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+8 & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ -2{x}^{2}+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/33/336a1503663239debacda06ca200e47151493ffbc94c3b797afb274c3355db97.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=-3{x}^{2}+8.$\n\nWe can now s