 and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x{e}^{y}}{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<Y\\,|\\,X=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)) & =P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)<Y\\,|\\,X=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3/2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right))\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3/2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{e}^{y}]}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3/2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186594229 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}2x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x{[{e}^{y