)-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(-{x}^{2}-2)-(-x-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-(0-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186594232 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{xy+2}{4}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2xy)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}-(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}-\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{72}\\right)}{(0.5713)\\cdot (0.2887)} \\\\  & ≈0.08,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186594232 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\left[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{xy+2}{4}\\,\\text{d}y\\right]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2xy)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\left[\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\left[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left[\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}-\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{72}\\right)}{(0.5713)\\cdot (0.2887)} \\\\  & ≈0.08,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186594666 1074 "![](images/f7/f7468aaa11c79f4306b67a558f09cbf5d8a6a480b726e883ecb373d7e5660e37.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(kx+6)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=kx+6.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^