orms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}({L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+1}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s}$ term in $Y(s).$ By the  second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\pi .$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u(t-\\pi )f(t-\\pi ) \\\\  & =u(t-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[{e}^{-(t-\\pi )}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi )=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ we simplify the expression inside the brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}u(t-\\pi )[{e}^{-(t-\\pi )}-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}u(t-\\pi )({e}^{-(t-\\pi )}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=\\frac{1}{2}u(t-\\pi )\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-(t-\\pi )}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186599807 1074 "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-\\pi s}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)(s+1)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+1)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+1),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)(s+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A({s}^{2}+1)+(Bs+C)(s+1)\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A((-1)^{2}+1)+0\\,⟹\\,2A=1\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n1=A+C\\,⟹\\,C=1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{-s+1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left({L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+1}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s}$ term in $Y(s).$ By the  second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\pi .$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u(t-\\pi )f(t-\\pi ) \\\\  & =u(t-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[{e}^{-(t-\\pi )}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi )=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ we simplify the expression inside the brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}u(t-\\pi )[{e}^{-(t-\\pi )}-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}u(t-\\pi )({e}^{-(t-\\pi )}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=\\frac{1}{2}u(t-\\pi )\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-(t-\\pi )}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186599811 1074 "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1=(x-1)(x+1).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+1)}=\\frac{A}{(x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n1=A(x-1)+B(x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A(1-1)+B(1+1) \\\\ 1 & =2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A(-1-1)+B(-1+1) \\\\ 1 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}=-\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-1}{x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\