)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\pi \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\pi )\\cdot (-\\pi )-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186601452 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\pi \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\pi )\\cdot (-\\pi )-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186601453 1071 "The position $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $r(t)=\\left\\langle -\\frac{2}{t},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,2\\right\\rangle ,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the tangential component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186601453 1071 "The position $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $r(t)=⟨-\\frac{2}{t},\