aligned}v & =\\int a\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2}{t}^{-1/2}+1,\\,{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{-2/3}\\right\\rangle \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\int \\left(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{-1/2}+1\\right)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\left({e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{-2/3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{1/2}+t,\\,{e}^{t}+{t}^{1/3}⟩+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a (vector) constant of integration. \n\nWe can solve for $C$ by substituting $v=⟨2,\\,1⟩\\,\\text{m/s}$ and $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2,\\,1⟩ & =⟨{1}^{1/2}+1,\\,{e}^{1}+{1}^{1/3}⟩+C \\\\ ⟨2,\\,1⟩ & =⟨2,\\,e+1⟩+C \\\\ C & =⟨0,\\,-e⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨{t}^{1/2}+t,\\,{e}^{t}+{t}^{1/3}⟩+⟨0,\\,-e⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{t}+t,\\,{e}^{t}+\\sqrt[3]{t}-e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186603021 1074 "Newton's second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nThe resultant force $F$ acting on $P$ equals the sum of the forces acting on $P.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & ={F}_{1}+{F}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t}},\\,6{e}^{t}⟩+⟨6,\\,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t}}+6,\\,6{e}^{t}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{-1/2}+6,\\,6{e}^{t}+2{t}^{-2/3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy Newton's second law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{F}{m} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot ⟨3{t}^{-1/2}+6,\\,6{e}^{t}+2{t}^{-2/3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{-1/2}+1,\\,{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{-2/3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the velocity vector $v,$ we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\int a\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{-1/2}+1,\\,{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{-2/3}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{-1/2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int ({e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{