pressed as $[-\\frac{1}{8},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{8}]\\cap [-\\frac{1}{8},\\infty )=\\left[-\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{8}\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in \\left[-\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{8}\\right],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{1}{8}\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{8}.$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186604618 1071 "\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{x})=\n$$\n\n" 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186604618 1071 "\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{x}\\right)=\n$$\n\n" 13194139545759 17592186576411 true] [17592186604621 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00bceda1bdf4f515646b3a1e8476f1eadde721aa52dbc9be9c7e866bcab99d3b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545759 17592186576411 false] [17592186604621 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00bceda1bdf4f515646b3a1e8476f1eadde721aa52dbc9be9c7e866bcab99d3b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\